NNAAMMEE
       mkfs - build a Linux filesystem

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
       mmkkffss [options] [--tt _t_y_p_e] [_f_s_-_o_p_t_i_o_n_s] _d_e_v_i_c_e [_s_i_z_e]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       TThhiiss mmkkffss ffrroonntteenndd iiss ddeepprreeccaatteedd iinn ffaavvoouurr ooff ffiilleessyysstteemm ssppeecciiffiicc
       mmkkffss..<<ttyyppee>> uuttiillss..

       mmkkffss is used to build a Linux filesystem on a device, usually a hard
       disk partition. The _d_e_v_i_c_e argument is either the device name (e.g.,
       _/_d_e_v_/_h_d_a_1, _/_d_e_v_/_s_d_b_2), or a regular file that shall contain the
       filesystem. The _s_i_z_e argument is the number of blocks to be used for
       the filesystem.

       The exit status returned by mmkkffss is 0 on success and 1 on failure.

       In actuality, mmkkffss is simply a front-end for the various filesystem
       builders (mmkkffss.._f_s_t_y_p_e) available under Linux. The filesystem-specific
       builder is searched for via your PPAATTHH environment setting only. Please
       see the filesystem-specific builder manual pages for further details.

OOPPTTIIOONNSS
       --tt, ----ttyyppee _t_y_p_e
           Specify the _t_y_p_e of filesystem to be built. If not specified, the
           default filesystem type (currently ext2) is used.

       _f_s_-_o_p_t_i_o_n_s
           Filesystem-specific options to be passed to the real filesystem
           builder.

       --VV, ----vveerrbboossee
           Produce verbose output, including all filesystem-specific commands
           that are executed. Specifying this option more than once inhibits
           execution of any filesystem-specific commands. This is really only
           useful for testing.

       --hh, ----hheellpp
           Display help text and exit.

       --VV, ----vveerrssiioonn
           Print version and exit. (Option --VV will display version information
           only when it is the only parameter, otherwise it will work as
           ----vveerrbboossee.)

BBUUGGSS
       All generic options must precede and not be combined with
       filesystem-specific options. Some filesystem-specific programs do not
       automatically detect the device size and require the _s_i_z_e parameter to
       be specified.

